<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khabur River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khabur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khabur River is a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Its valley was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a> and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washukanni">Washukanni</a>. After the Hittite defeat, Mitanni's remaining territory was confined to the Khabur valley as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanigalbat">Hanigalbat</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khabur-river.jpg" alt="Khabur River" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khabur,SheikhHamad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the twenty-fourth century BCE it was the capital of a kingdom that controlled many villages and towns, dealing on equal terms with Mari on the Euphrates and Nagar in the <b>Khabur</b> region, and in contact with Kish in Mesopotamia and even Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Zimri-Lim regained Mari around 1775 BCE with the help of Yarim-Lim of Yamhad and fought to control the <b>Khabur</b> plains and the tribes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mitanni Empire emerged around the 16th century BCE in northern Mesopotamia and the <b>Khabur River</b> valley, in what is now north-eastern Syria and south-eastern Turkey, with an influence reaching into northern Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Mediterranean coast and the mountains of the west across the fertile valleys of the Euphrates, Orontes and <b>Khabur</b> to the eastern desert, with Damascus and Aleppo among the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world and a people of Sunni Arabs, Alawites, Druze, Christians, Kurds and Armenians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 3rd and early 2nd millennia BCE the same gods appear in the personal names of the Amorites, whose homeland lay in the middle Euphrates and lower <b>Khabur</b> regions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Shubat-Enlil, at Tell Leilan in the <b>Khabur</b> plains, was the old city of Shehna, renamed by Shamshi-Adad as the capital of his Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/state/apum">Apum</a></li></ul></section>
